I have an idea for a set of insects that would be significantly more
dangerous than the current ones (and therefore would occur only later
in the game).  The one thing they'd all have in common (besides
generally being stronger than the insects already in the game) is the
ability to reproduce (i.e., create a few more of their own kind)
whenever they successfully eat.  So they look for food, and if they
find any, they eat and spawn; spawning uses up nutrition, so then
they'd be looking for food again.  Spawning WOULD increment the born
counter and respect extinction.

There would be several different types of these insects,
differentiated mainly by what they eat.  All of them would be rather
fast (probably faster than a typical player in speed boots).

Locusts (a), would eat only vegan items (comestibles, but they also
eat vegan monsters alive, e.g., lichens) and in general would be the
wimpiest of the lot; you could expect to start seeing them before
Medusa, possibly even soon after Delphi.  The one time when locusts
would be a particularly grave threat would be if you (or your pets)
are polymorphed into a vegan monster, in which case they'd be almost
as much of a threat as scarab beetles (see below).  Locusts can fly
and are small enough to slip under doors.  They will follow you to
other levels ONLY if you are food to them (i.e., polymorphed into
something vegan).  Locust corpses would be non-vegetarian and
otherwise unremarkable.

Roaches (a) would eat corpses (including rotten ones, also including
roach corpses, but NOT corpses that provide zero nutrition, such as
wraith) and prepared foods (rations, candy bars, cream pies, pancakes,
etc) and be harder to kill than locusts.  Roaches can slip under doors
and get into closed and locked containers, so e.g. if you lock a
corpse or some rood rations in a chest and there's a roach on the
level you could open the chest later and find a large number of
roaches.  Given enough time, they can also get out of chests on their
own (small chance per turn per roach), so leaving them in there does
not dispose of them.  Roaches would not as a rule be able to get into
containers that you are carrying, but if you leave your bag on the
floor for a few turns all bets are off.  Roach corpses would have the
same edibility profile as tripe:  cavemen for instance could eat
them.  Another way to get rid of them (so they don't get eaten by
other roaches, resulting in new live roaches) would be to put them in
an icebox:  roaches don't get into iceboxes.  (You could also
polymorph them or employ other item destruction tactics.)

Scarab beetles (x) would eat live prey, especially you (and your
pets), but also anything that attacks them (e.g., due to conflict) and
possibly the occasional peaceful monster from time to time.  Their
melee attack would involve taking bites out of you (reducing your
strength, max hit points, weight, and possibly also constitution), and
if they do so successfully they gain nutrition and are thus able to
spawn more scarab beetles, placing you in danger of becoming
surrounded by the things.  Scarab beetles would be more dangerous than
locusts and roaches and would typically not begin to appear until
after you enter Gehennom.  They can fly and are small enough to slip
under doors and will follow you from one level to another.  They also
have infravision and can locate you by sound if you are not stealthy.
(Locating you by sound works around corners and through walls, but
only within a fixed radius.)

Eating a scarab beetle corpse would have a small percentage chance
(each time) of turning you into a mummy if there is a corresponding
mummy type for your current race (human => human mummy, etc.)   Unlike
falling into a polymorph trap, this change would not automatically
wear off in time.


